1
00:00:05,339 --> 00:00:15,840
now the question is we have this type of devices
and they are connected us we just saw the

2
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:21,970
question is how can the processor give commands
and data to the specific controller when it wants

3
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:23,980
to do I/O?

4
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:34,230
so there are basically two ways to go the first way
to go here is to have basically direct communication

5
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:42,410
with the controllers so the CPU in this case
is reading and writing directly into the registers

6
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:50,379
the small memories of the controllers with
special instructions that allow the CPU to directly

7
00:00:50,379 --> 00:00:54,079
communicate with the devices

8
00:00:54,079 --> 00:01:00,620
so if the CPU wants to issue a request
to read something from the disk then it will

9
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:07,579
go and read and write registers that belong
to the controller that is the one behind which

10
00:01:07,579 --> 00:01:09,590
you have the disks

11
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:16,850
the alternative way is to use and this is
what is commonly used memory mapped I/O

12
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:24,939
in this case the registers of the controllers
are mapped into the main memory so in the address

13
00:01:24,939 --> 00:01:31,079
space of the processor that are special parts of the main
memory that are actually mirrors

14
00:01:31,079 --> 00:01:37,979
if you want of the registers of the different
devices now then the CPU writes and reads only

15
00:01:37,979 --> 00:01:47,049
main memory but when it writes or reads to specific
portions of it what is doing is actually writing

16
00:01:47,049 --> 00:01:50,939
and reading to the registers of specific controllers

17
00:01:50,939 --> 00:01:59,649
you can also use at the same time both ways
of communication depending on the devices

18
00:01:59,649 --> 00:02:08,770
you want to communicate with so this is version
one here the CPU has dedicated channels physically

19
00:02:08,770 --> 00:02:21,409
wires that are used the allow the CPU to run dedicated
instructions that can write or read specific bytes

20
00:02:21,409 --> 00:02:23,280
in the memory of the controller

21
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:30,730
so in this case the CPU needs to have special
instructions what it wants for example to

22
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:39,099
read or write from the disk or read and write from the 
network card and so on the alternative is to rely on someone

23
00:02:39,099 --> 00:02:43,069
sitting in the middle between the CPU and
the different controllers

24
00:02:43,069 --> 00:02:50,769
and this will be usually the motherboard to
memory-mapped I/O so in this case parts as I said

25
00:02:50,769 --> 00:02:56,400
of the main memory are mirrors of the registers
of the devices and it's the motherboard that

26
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:03,049
takes care of keeping these copies consistent
so that whenever the CPU writes or reads into memory

27
00:03:03,049 --> 00:03:07,980
actually these the information that is
being written

28
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:14,189
into the registers is moved also to the controller
when the controller updates this information

29
00:03:14,189 --> 00:03:23,290
that information is also brought back to that main memory 
for the CPU to see that the information has changed

30
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:28,580
an example a classic example is
the graphics controller

31
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:37,230
there is a significantly large part of the
memory that is used to hold the screen contents

32
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:42,769
this is memory-mapped I/O the processor
sends the output for the screen what you are

33
00:03:42,769 --> 00:03:47,749
going to see on the screen right in this special
region of the memory

34
00:03:47,749 --> 00:03:54,790
and then the motherboard takes care of sharing
this data with the graphics controller that translates

35
00:03:54,790 --> 00:04:01,500
that into the thing that you actually see
on screen and this is just an example of the

36
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:10,110
specific portions of memory which are reserved
for a specific controller for example for

37
00:04:10,110 --> 00:04:17,200
this controller and so on for the graphics controller and so on

38
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:25,430
now the registers you have usually are the following
ones for the controllers you have to data in register

39
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:32,199
this is read by the host to get input and
the data out register which is written by

40
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:37,430
the host to send output the host here would be the CPU

41
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:47,580
and then you have the status registers that contain bits
that can be read by the host these are bits indicating

42
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:53,770
for example whether the current command has completed 
whether a byte is available to be read or whether or a device

43
00:04:53,770 --> 00:04:58,620
error has occurred and so it depends of course on the type
of controller you are communicating with

44
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:05,530
you have control registers to start commands or to
change the mode of a device so let's see

45
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:11,390
for example you can have a certain bit in
the control register of a set of serial port to chose

46
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:15,669
between full duplex or only haf-duplex for the communication

47
00:05:15,669 --> 00:05:22,270
so one direction on both directions at the
same time or you can have bits that are set

48
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:30,990
by the host to specify the word length let's say to seven or
eight bits that are supported by a specific

49
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:31,560
serial port

